,0.9998],"b":[0.0215,0.9998]},{"at":[0.9286,0.3571],"a":[-0.0215,0.9998],"b":[0.0215,0.9998]},{"at":[0.9286,0.5],"a":[-0.0215,0.9998],"b":[0.0215,0.9998]},{"at":[0.9286,0.6429],"a":[-0.0215,0.9998],"b":[0.0215,0.9998]},{"at":[0.9286,0.7857],"a":[-0.0215,0.9998],"b":[0.0215,0.9998]},{"at":[0.9286,0.9286],"a":[-0.0215,0.9998],"b":[0.0215,0.9998]}],"markers":[],"hatch":[],"settings":"$m = 1$, $b = 2 \\sqrt{2}$, $r = 4$, $\\phi = 0$","input":null,"caption":["The plane of $t$ and $\\theta$ ($r = 4m$, $\\phi = 0$) of Bonnor's magnetic dipole ($m = 1$, $b = 2\\sqrt{2}\\,m$), the axis from one black hole to the other, where $Z = 0$ and the strut stands. A ray along it has $c\\,dt/d\\theta = \\pm Y^2/((m^2 + b^2)^{3/2}\\sin^3\\theta)$, and every rotation about the axis fixes the plane, so the rays are null geodesics.","The two black holes are the edges $\\theta = 0$ and $\\theta = \\pi$, where the cones close. Light sent along the strut from $\\theta = \\pi/2$ takes an infinite time $t$ to reach either hole, and the distance to each is infinite as well, the length of the throat of an extremal black hole."],"source":{"fields":["coords","parameters","metric_components","inverse_metric_components","kretschmann","domains"],"version":"499182993fe23686"},"slices":[{"view":"equator","surface":0,"label":"$t = 0$, the equatorial plane","version":"3e57f564f08d80b7","lines":[],"points":[[0.5,0.5]],"fills":[],"place":{"at":[0.5,0.5],"anchor":"bl","dx":5.1,"dy":-5.1}}]}]}}
